:: StoryTitle
Once Saved

:: StoryData
{
  "ifid": "<generated by Twine>",
  "format": "Inkjot",
  "format-version": "0.6.0",
  "start": "porch"
}

:: Inklink [config]
(((
Ex 13: a debate `game`, solo. Thanksgiving, on the back porch after the
pie: your cousin Ben, who teaches Sunday school at a Baptist church, holds
that a man truly saved can never be lost. You hold, with the Council of
Trent, that the justified can lose grace by mortal sin, and that
perseverance to the end is a gift no one may promise himself. Neither of
you is angry. Both of you mean to win.

A draft for milestone 23, written before the words it needs exist. It is
an encounter that is not a fight: objections are threats, answers are
counters, and what is at stake is two values on Ben (how sure he is, and
how well he understands you) and one on you (how well your case stands).

Ben's side is argued at its strongest: the texts his churches lead with,
in the King James. Yours is from Trent's decree on justification (1547),
St Thomas, and Cornelius à Lapide, with the Douay-Rheims. A win should
mean something.

New words it asks for (see `examples/drafts/README.md`):

| Word | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `encounter ben ~ as debate` | What kind of exchange this is: a label for conditions, the platform and reports, not separate rules. `debate` is proposed beside the decided `fight`, `negotiation`, `seduction`, `discovery`, `other` |
| `pattern … ~ by weight` | The opponent's next move is the heaviest whose `when` holds, not the next in a fixed order; `~ each once` uses each up |
| `x first when …` | As in Ex 2: a rebuttal comes before the next objection, while it holds |
| `~ answered by scripture, distinction` | The counters that answer a threat may be any tags, not only the four of a fight |
| `answer ben` | The Link's act answers Ben's threat this round, if one of its tags is in the threat's `answered by` (outcome `answered`) |
| `threat.answered_with` | In `[resolve]`: the tag that answered it |
| `landed` | The outcome once called `hit`: the threat went home |
| `unleash x ~ by y` | A threat from outside the pattern: Carol's question, from the kitchen door. It takes the round's place |
| `on round begins`, `encounter.round`, `encounter.over` | The moments and the count of an encounter's rounds |
| `ben.objections.done` | A pattern `~ each once` with nothing left to choose |
| `hint: "…" ~ when …` | A hint offered only while its condition holds |
)))
kind: game
tags: debate ~ theology ~ family ~ thanksgiving
ages: 12..
mode: solo
pov: visitor
start: porch

character visitor: "you" ~ selectable ~ custom ~ pronouns chosen
~ state footing: 3 ~ shown as "how well your case stands"
npc ben: "Ben" ~ pronouns he ~ in porch
npc carol: "Carol" ~ pronouns she ~ in kitchen

place porch: "the back porch"
place kitchen: "the kitchen"

(((
Hints are what Ben has said before: at Easter, at Grandpa's funeral, in
the group chat. No Inquests, and no rewinds: an argument is not taken back.
)))
resource hints: 3 ~ shown as "things he said before"

on round begins when encounter.round == 4
~ then unleash carols_question ~ by carol
~ then play kitchen_door

(((
What you can say. Answers are general moves; what they say depends on
what he raised (see `porch:after`). Each is tagged with the kind of answer
it is: Ben's objections say which kinds answer them.
)))
act distinguish: cost 0 ~ distinction ~ "draw a distinction"
act scripture: cost 0 ~ scripture ~ "answer with Scripture"
act reason: cost 0 ~ logic ~ "follow his premise to where it leads"
act liken: cost 0 ~ analogy ~ "offer a likeness"
act cite: cost 0 ~ authority ~ "say what the Church teaches, and where"
act grant: cost 0 ~ concede ~ "grant what is true in it"

(((
Your own case: each point is pressed once, and costs you the round, so
the objection he has just raised goes unanswered.
)))
act press_hebrews_six: cost 0 ~ press ~ "Hebrews 6:4-6"
act press_hebrews_ten: cost 0 ~ press ~ "Hebrews 10:26"
act press_john_fifteen: cost 0 ~ press ~ "John 15:6"
act press_romans_eleven: cost 0 ~ press ~ "Romans 11:20-22"
act press_ezekiel: cost 0 ~ press ~ "Ezekiel 18:24"
act press_prodigal: cost 0 ~ press ~ "Luke 15:24"
act press_paul: cost 0 ~ press ~ "1 Corinthians 9:27"

:: ben_argues [npc]
(((
How sure he is: 6, once saved always saved; 0, he will go home and read
Hebrews again. How well he understands you: 0, he thinks you believe in
salvation by works; 5, he could state your view in words you would sign.
)))
--
stat conviction: 6 ~ 0..=6
stat understanding: 0 ~ 0..=5

pattern objections: john_ten, romans_eight, sealed, by_works, assurance, never_of_us ~ by weight ~ each once ~ one per round
~ hypothetical first when you.did(press_hebrews_six)
~ false_branches first when you.did(press_john_fifteen)
~ corporate first when you.did(press_romans_eleven)
~ still_a_son first when you.did(press_prodigal)

threat john_ten: at visitor ~ answered by distinction, scripture, authority ~ weight 6
~ then lower target.footing 1
threat romans_eight: at visitor ~ answered by distinction, scripture, authority ~ weight 5
~ then lower target.footing 1
threat sealed: at visitor ~ answered by scripture, analogy, authority ~ weight 4
~ then lower target.footing 1
threat by_works: at visitor ~ answered by distinction, scripture, concede, authority ~ weight 5 ~ when ben.understanding <= 2
~ then lower target.footing 1
threat assurance: at visitor ~ answered by distinction, scripture, concede, authority ~ weight 3
~ then lower target.footing 1
threat never_of_us: at visitor ~ answered by scripture, logic, authority ~ weight 4
~ then lower target.footing 1

(((
His rebuttals: they come when you press the point they answer.
)))
threat hypothetical: at visitor ~ answered by distinction, authority
~ then lower target.footing 1
threat false_branches: at visitor ~ answered by logic, scripture
~ then lower target.footing 1
threat corporate: at visitor ~ answered by scripture, distinction
~ then lower target.footing 1
threat still_a_son: at visitor ~ answered by scripture, concede
~ then lower target.footing 1

(((
From the kitchen door, once, halfway through: not Ben's, and not a round's.
)))
threat carols_question: at visitor ~ answered by analogy, concede
~ then lower target.footing 1

:: porch
(((
The encounter ends when he has nothing left to raise, or one of you is
done: his conviction gone, or your case in pieces.
)))
encounter ben
~ as debate
~ round by round
~ acts last
~ until ben.objections.done or ben.conviction == 0 or visitor.footing == 0
--
The porch light is the only light for a mile. Inside, someone has started the dishes, and the football is on with nobody watching it.

Ben puts his feet up on the rail. “So. Aunt Ruth says you think a man can be saved on Sunday and damned by Friday.”

“Not quite,” you say.

“Then tell me what you think,” he says, “and I’ll tell you why it’s wrong. Nicely.”
> [[“All right.”->porch:round]] ~ id begin

:: porch:round [round]
let next: ben.objections.next
hint: "At Grandpa’s funeral he said: “Once in His hand, always in His hand. Nobody can take him out.” He means nobody at all." ~ when next == john_ten
hint: "In the group chat he posted Romans 8 with “NOTHING” in capitals. Notice what the list leaves out." ~ when next == romans_eight
hint: "At Easter he called the Spirit “God’s down payment.” Ask him what Ephesians 4:30 tells you not to do to the seal." ~ when next == sealed
hint: "He thinks you believe in earning it. Say first what you agree on: grace, all grace, from first to last." ~ when next == by_works
hint: "He says he knows he is saved. Ask what Paul said he knew about himself." ~ when next == assurance
hint: "His answer to every apostate is that they never really believed. Ask whether he could ever tell, from the inside." ~ when next == never_of_us
--
[if next == john_ten]
“John ten,” Ben says. “*My sheep hear my voice, and I know them, and they follow me: and I give unto them eternal life; and they shall never perish, neither shall any man pluck them out of my hand.* Never. Eternal. If you can lose it, it wasn’t eternal, it was temporary life with good marketing.”

[if next == romans_eight]
“Romans eight. *Neither death, nor life, nor angels, nor principalities, nor powers, nor things present, nor things to come, nor height, nor depth, nor any other creature, shall be able to separate us from the love of God.* Paul names everything there is. What’s left to separate you?”

[if next == sealed]
“You were *sealed with that holy Spirit of promise, which is the earnest of our inheritance.* Ephesians one. The earnest. The deposit. God put His own Spirit down as a guarantee. Does God forfeit His own deposit?”

[if next == by_works]
“Here’s my real problem.” He takes his feet off the rail. “If you can lose it by sinning, you keep it by not sinning. And that’s works. *By grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: not of works, lest any man should boast.* You’ve got Jesus starting it and you finishing it.”

[if next == assurance]
“First John five. *These things have I written unto you that believe on the name of the Son of God; that ye may know that ye have eternal life.* Know. Not hope, not guess. Can you say you know where you’d go tonight?”

[if next == never_of_us]
“And the people who fall away? John answers that. *They went out from us, but they were not of us; for if they had been of us, they would no doubt have continued with us.* Somebody who leaves shows he was never in.”

[if next == hypothetical]
“Hebrews six.” He’s ready for it. “Read it again. *It is impossible … if they shall fall away, to renew them again unto repentance.* Impossible. If that’s about losing salvation, then nobody who loses it can ever get it back, and you don’t believe that either, you’ve got confession. So it’s a hypothetical. A warning that works by being a warning.”

[if next == false_branches]
“John fifteen’s branches are people who are attached on the outside. Judas was one of the twelve. In the vine, not of the vine.”

[if next == corporate]
“Romans eleven is about Gentiles and Jews, the whole people. It’s branches as nations, not you and me.”

[if next == still_a_son]
“The prodigal never stopped being a son. That’s my whole point. He was in a pigpen and he was still his father’s boy.”

[end]
> ~ cost 0
> [[Draw a distinction->porch:after]] ~ distinguish ~ then answer ben
> [[Answer with Scripture->porch:after]] ~ scripture ~ then answer ben
> [[Follow his premise to where it leads->porch:after]] ~ reason ~ then answer ben
> [[Offer a likeness->porch:after]] ~ liken ~ then answer ben
> [[Say what the Church teaches->porch:after]] ~ cite ~ then answer ben
> [[Grant what is true in it->porch:after]] ~ grant ~ then answer ben

> ~ cost 0
> [[Press Hebrews 6->porch:after]] ~ press_hebrews_six ~ once ~ then lower ben.conviction 1
> [[Press Hebrews 10->porch:after]] ~ press_hebrews_ten ~ once ~ then lower ben.conviction 1
> [[Press John 15->porch:after]] ~ press_john_fifteen ~ once ~ then lower ben.conviction 1
> [[Press Romans 11->porch:after]] ~ press_romans_eleven ~ once ~ then lower ben.conviction 1
> [[Press Ezekiel 18->porch:after]] ~ press_ezekiel ~ once ~ then lower ben.conviction 1
> [[Press the prodigal son->porch:after]] ~ press_prodigal ~ once ~ then lower ben.conviction 1
> [[Press Paul on himself->porch:after]] ~ press_paul ~ once ~ then lower ben.conviction 1

:: porch:after [resolve]
(((
Scoring, by what answered him. A good answer moves him; citing the Church,
or granting his point, helps him understand you without moving him; an
objection left standing costs you.
)))
lower ben.conviction 1 when threat.outcome == answered and threat.answered_with in [distinction, scripture, logic, analogy]
raise ben.understanding 1 when threat.outcome == answered
raise ben.understanding 1 when threat.outcome == answered and threat.answered_with in [authority, concede]
--
[if you.did(press_hebrews_six)]
You read it to him: *For it is impossible for those who were once illuminated, have tasted also the heavenly gift, and were made partakers of the Holy Ghost … and are fallen away: to be renewed again to penance.* “Illuminated. Tasted. Partakers of the Holy Ghost. That’s not a man who was never saved.”

[if you.did(press_hebrews_ten)]
*For if we sin wilfully after having the knowledge of the truth, there is now left no sacrifice for sins.* “We. He puts himself in it.”

[if you.did(press_john_fifteen)]
*If any one abide not in me, he shall be cast forth as a branch, and shall wither.* “Abide means remain. You can’t remain where you never were.”

[if you.did(press_romans_eleven)]
*See then the goodness and the severity of God … towards thee, the goodness of God, if thou abide in goodness, otherwise thou also shalt be cut off.* “If thou abide. Otherwise.”

[if you.did(press_ezekiel)]
*But if the just man turn himself away from his justice, and do iniquity … in them he shall die.* “The just man. Not a man who only looked just.”

[if you.did(press_prodigal)]
“The father says it twice: *this my son was dead, and is come to life again: was lost, and is found.* Dead, and his son. Both.”

[if you.did(press_paul)]
*I chastise my body, and bring it into subjection: lest perhaps, when I have preached to others, I myself should become a castaway.* “If Paul could say that about himself, can you or I be surer than Paul?”

[if threat.outcome == answered and threat.name == john_ten and threat.answered_with == distinction]
“No one can pluck them out,” you say. “No one else. It says nothing about the sheep that stops hearing and stops following. Read the verse before: *my sheep hear my voice … and they follow me.* It’s the ones who follow.”

[if threat.outcome == answered and threat.name == john_ten and threat.answered_with == scripture]
“Same Gospel, five chapters on: *If any one abide not in me, he shall be cast forth as a branch.* The same Lord who says no one can snatch them says a branch can be cut off. Both are true.”

[if threat.outcome == answered and threat.name == romans_eight and threat.answered_with == distinction]
“Every item on that list is something outside you. Death, angels, powers, things to come. He doesn’t list *you*. Nothing can drag you away from God. You can walk.”

[if threat.outcome == answered and threat.name == romans_eight and threat.answered_with == scripture]
“Same chapter, verse thirteen: *if you live according to the flesh, you shall die: but if by the Spirit you mortify the deeds of the flesh, you shall live.* He’s writing to Christians. *You shall die.*”

[if threat.outcome == answered and threat.name == sealed and threat.answered_with == scripture]
“Ephesians four: *grieve not the holy Spirit of God: whereby you are sealed unto the day of redemption.* The seal is real, and you can grieve it. He wouldn’t warn you off something that couldn’t happen.”

[if threat.outcome == answered and threat.name == sealed and threat.answered_with == analogy]
“A ring is a pledge too,” you say. “It’s given for good. It doesn’t marry you to someone who has walked out of the house. God doesn’t take back the pledge. A man can still leave the marriage.”

[if threat.outcome == answered and threat.name == by_works and threat.answered_with == concede]
“Grace, from first to last,” you say. “Trent says it outright: we are justified freely, because nothing that comes before justification, faith or works, merits the grace of it. We agree there. And the Council calls perseverance itself a gift.”

[if threat.outcome == answered and threat.name == by_works and threat.answered_with == distinction]
“Throwing away a gift isn’t the same as earning it. I can’t earn my way in. I can walk out. Losing grace by mortal sin isn’t failing a test; it’s turning it down.”

[if threat.outcome == answered and threat.name == by_works and threat.answered_with == scripture]
“*With fear and trembling work out your salvation. For it is God who worketh in you, both to will and to accomplish.* Both halves in one breath. Work it out; God is the one working.”

[if threat.outcome == answered and threat.name == assurance and threat.answered_with == concede]
“I can say I hope,” you say, “firmly. Trent tells us to put a most firm hope in God’s help. What it won’t let anyone promise himself, without a revelation, is that he will persevere to the end.”

[if threat.outcome == answered and threat.name == assurance and threat.answered_with == distinction]
“Knowing you have eternal life now isn’t knowing you’ll keep the faith till you die. John says the first. Paul refuses the second for himself: *I am not conscious to myself of any thing, yet am I not hereby justified.*”

[if threat.outcome == answered and threat.name == assurance and threat.answered_with == scripture]
“*He that thinketh himself to stand, let him take heed lest he fall.* Paul wrote that to a church.”

[if threat.outcome == answered and threat.name == never_of_us and threat.answered_with == logic]
“Then you can’t know you’re saved either,” you say. “If the man who falls away in twenty years was never saved, he felt exactly as saved as you do now. Your test only works backward, from the grave.”

[if threat.outcome == answered and threat.name == never_of_us and threat.answered_with == scripture]
“Peter: *if, flying from the pollutions of the world, through the knowledge of our Lord and Saviour Jesus Christ, they be again entangled in them and overcome, their latter state is become unto them worse than the former.* They escaped. Through knowing Christ. Then they went back.”

[if threat.outcome == answered and threat.name == hypothetical and threat.answered_with == distinction]
“Impossible for *us* to renew them,” you say. “The writer means they can’t be baptised again, can’t start over from the font: *once illuminated* is baptism. À Lapide says all the Greek Fathers read it so, and most of the Latins. Not that God can’t raise them; that is what penance is for. Look at verse nine: *we trust better things of you.* You don’t warn people off a cliff that isn’t there.”

[if threat.outcome == answered and threat.name == false_branches and threat.answered_with == logic]
“He says *in me* of the branch that’s cut off, the same words he uses of the branch that bears fruit. If the first is only on the outside, so is the second.”

[if threat.outcome == answered and threat.name == corporate and threat.answered_with == scripture]
“Verse twenty: *thou standest by faith: be not highminded, but fear.* Thou. One person, standing by faith. You don’t stand by your nation’s faith.”

[if threat.outcome == answered and threat.name == still_a_son and threat.answered_with == concede]
“He stayed a son,” you say. “We’d say baptism marks you for good. And his father still calls him dead. That’s what we mean by mortal sin: a son who is dead, until he comes home.”

[if threat.outcome == answered and threat.answered_with == authority]
“Trent says it in so many words: grace is lost by every mortal sin, not only by losing the faith; and no one, without a special revelation, can be certain he will persevere. St Thomas says even the justified must pray for perseverance, because it is a further help of God.” Ben nods slowly. He doesn’t accept the Council, but he can see now what you aren’t saying.

[if threat.outcome == landed]
You start to answer, and stop. Ben lets the silence sit, kindly, which is worse.

[if threat.name == carols_question and threat.outcome == answered]
“It’s like being married,” you tell Carol. “He doesn’t stop being faithful to me. I could still walk out. I’m not planning on it.” Carol laughs, and Ben looks at you differently.

[end]
> [[Go on->porch:round]] ~ cost 0 ~ when not encounter.over
> [[Let it rest->ending]] ~ cost 0 ~ when encounter.over

:: kitchen_door
(((
Halfway through, Carol leans out of the kitchen door with a dish towel.
Not one of Ben's: a threat let loose from outside the pattern, which
takes this round's place (Ben lets her ask).
)))
--
“Can I ask something?” Carol says. “If somebody we love stops believing, was he never saved, or did he lose it? Because I’d like to know which one to pray for.”

:: ending
(((
Outcome tiers: defend your position (your case still stands), convince
him (his certainty shaken), both, a tie, or a loss. How well he
understands you colours each.
)))
--
[if visitor.footing >= 2 and ben.conviction <= 2]
{embed nib: "both"}

[if visitor.footing >= 2 and ben.conviction > 2]
{embed nib: "defended"}

[if visitor.footing < 2 and ben.conviction <= 2]
{embed nib: "convinced"}

[if visitor.footing == 1 and ben.conviction > 2]
{embed nib: "tie"}

[if visitor.footing == 0]
{embed nib: "lost"}

:: both [ending]
--
Ben is quiet for a long time. “I’m going to read Hebrews again,” he says finally. “Don’t look so pleased.” You weren’t, much. You were thinking that he argued it better than most priests you know.

:: defended [ending]
--
“I don’t agree with you,” Ben says. “But you don’t believe what I thought you believed.”

[if ben.understanding >= 4]
He says it back to you, in his own words: grace from first to last, a gift you can refuse, a hope that is firm but not a promise. You would sign it.

:: convinced [ending]
--
You lost the thread more than once tonight, and he caught you every time. And still, at the end, he says: “I don’t know. Peter, and Hebrews. I don’t know.” He sounds younger than he is.

:: tie [ending]
--
“Same time next year?” Ben says. “Bring better verses.” You both laugh. Neither of you has moved an inch, and you like each other more than you did at dinner.

:: lost [ending]
--
Ben is gentle about it, which is the worst part. You go in to help with the dishes and spend the whole drive home thinking of what you should have said.

:: a_good_answer_moves_him [test]
start: porch
do: "“All right.”"
expect: ben.objections.next == john_ten
do: "Draw a distinction"
expect: threat.outcome == answered and threat.answered_with == distinction
expect: ben.conviction == 5 and ben.understanding == 1
expect: visitor.footing == 3

:: citing_the_council_explains_but_does_not_move [test]
start: porch
do: "“All right.”"
do: "Say what the Church teaches"
expect: ben.conviction == 6 and ben.understanding == 2

:: the_wrong_kind_of_answer_lets_it_land [test]
start: porch
do: "“All right.”"
do: "Offer a likeness"
expect: threat.outcome == landed
expect: visitor.footing == 2

:: pressing_a_point_brings_his_rebuttal [test]
start: porch
do: "“All right.”"
do: "Press Hebrews 6"
expect: ben.conviction == 5 and visitor.footing == 2
do: "Go on"
expect: ben.objections.next == hypothetical

:: carol_asks_halfway [test]
start: porch
do: "“All right.”"
do: "Grant what is true in it"
do: "Go on"
do: "Grant what is true in it"
do: "Go on"
do: "Grant what is true in it"
do: "Go on"
expect: threat.name == carols_question
expect text: "which one to pray for"
